SQLite 3.53.4: let seeks continue from the cursor's current leaf page.

Applied by build.sh to the SQLite source tree before the amalgamation is generated.

1. src/vdbe.c, OP_OpenRead: a correlated subquery opens its read cursor once per outer
   row. When the cursor is already open on the same b-tree, keep the BtCursor and its
   position (resetting the VdbeCursor exactly as allocateCursor() does) instead of
   closing it and allocating a new one.
2. src/btree.c, sqlite3BtreeIndexMoveto(): the existing shortcut that searches the
   current leaf page instead of descending from the root only applies on the last page
   of the index. Also take it on any leaf page whose first cell is <= the key and whose
   last cell is > the key.
3. src/btree.c, sqlite3BtreeTableMoveto(): the same for table b-trees: when the current
   leaf page's first and last rowids bracket the key, search that page only (stock
   SQLite only special-cases key == current + 1).

Checked with SQLite's own test suite (veryquick, 330,400 tests, assertions on: the same
28 failing cases as unpatched SQLite, all permission tests that fail when run as root)
and a differential fuzzer against unpatched SQLite. Results are identical; seeks that
move to nearby keys skip the descent from the root.

